s, IAP and MusD/ETn, are major targets and are strongly inhibited by tRFs in retrotransposition assays. 22 nt tRFs post-transcriptionally silence coding-competent ERVs, while 18 nt tRFs specifically interfere with reverse transcription and retrotransposon mobility. The PBS offers a unique target to specifically inhibit LTR-retrotransposons and tRF-targeting is a potentially highly conserved mechanism of small RNA-mediated transposon control.</p></abstract><abstract abstract-type="graphical" id="Abs2"><title>eTOC BLURB</title><p id="P2">3&#8242; tRNA fragments limit the mobility of transposable elements in mouse ES cells</p><p id="P3">
